# Storage and Migrations

- Define the authoritative data invariant, transaction boundary, and failure
  behavior before changing persistence.
- Use parameterized queries and least-privilege access.
- Inspect indexes and query shape; reject accidental table scans or per-row
  queries at production scale.
- Make migrations compatible with mixed application versions when rolling
  deployment is possible.
- Separate schema expansion, backfill, traffic switch, and cleanup when one
  atomic migration would lock or rewrite large data.
- Make backfills resumable, observable, bounded, and safe to retry.
- Provide backup, rollback, or forward-repair reasoning for destructive schema
  or data transformations.
- Test constraints, duplicates, nulls, concurrency, interruption, and old data.
